// os.Args — the program's command-line arguments.  Not build-constrained: it
// rests entirely on bootstrap.Args(), which every target provides (the libc
// host captures the real argv in the C runtime's main; a bare-metal target,
// which has no command line, returns empty), so one definition serves all
// flavors.
package "pkg/std/os"

import "pkg/bootstrap"

// args is the program's command-line arguments, shaped like Go's os.Args
// (element 0 the program name, elements 1.. the arguments) and built ONCE at
// package initialization: argvWithProgName captures bootstrap.Args() and lays
// out the fresh managed slice here, so Args() is a cheap accessor that does no
// work.  A program-lifetime managed global; its readonly element type
// (@[]readonly @[]readonly char) means neither the slice nor the argument text
// it references can be mutated, so it is safe to hand the same shared slice to
// every caller (see Args).  A single empty element (the program-name
// placeholder) on a target with no command line.
var args @[]readonly @[]readonly char = argvWithProgName(bootstrap.Args())

// Args returns the program's command-line arguments, indexed like Go's os.Args:
// element 0 is the program name and elements 1.. are the arguments.  The result
// is a managed slice of readonly strings whose element slots are themselves
// readonly.
//
// The program name is currently a placeholder — an empty string — because no
// mechanism yet captures argv[0]: bootstrap.Args() deliberately returns only
// the arguments, not the program name, so element 0 has nothing real to hold.
// TODO: populate element 0 with the real program name once a bootstrap
// primitive exposes argv[0] (bootstrap.Args() cannot — its existing consumers
// rely on it being the args-only list); tracked in claude-todo.md.
//
// Args does no work and allocates nothing: it hands back the one slice built at
// package init, so every call returns the SAME cached slice.  That is safe to
// share because the type is fully readonly — a caller can read and reorder a
// private copy, but can neither reassign an element nor mutate the argument text
// of the shared slice.  The arguments are fixed at process start and do not
// track any later change.  On a target with no command line the result is a
// single empty element (the program-name placeholder).
func Args() @[]readonly @[]readonly char {
	return args
}

// argvWithProgName builds the Go-shaped argv from a raw argument list `src`
// (the arguments, EXCLUDING the program name): element 0 is the empty
// program-name placeholder (the zero-value slice make_slice leaves) and
// elements 1.. are readonly views of src.  It builds a mutable slice locally
// and returns it as the fully-readonly type.  Split out from the `args`
// initializer so it is unit-testable on a synthetic argument list, independent
// of the process's real argv.
func argvWithProgName(src @[]@[]char) @[]readonly @[]readonly char {
	var n int = len(src)
	// n+1: element 0 is reserved for the program name, left as the zero-value
	// empty slice (the placeholder); the real arguments follow at 1..n.  The
	// local is mutable so it can be filled; the return widens it to the
	// readonly element type.
	var out @[]@[]readonly char = make_slice(@[]readonly char, n + 1)
	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
		out[i + 1] = src[i]
	}
	return out
}
